Optimize the database.

00:37:14.680 --> 00:37:18.680
If object caching like Redis is not available for the WordPress site,

00:37:18.680 --> 00:37:21.680
requests for database content go straight to the database.

00:37:21.680 --> 00:37:26.680
First, the database checks its cache to serve the results directly, if possible,

00:37:26.680 --> 00:37:28.680
and stores it for future use.

00:37:28.680 --> 00:37:31.680
If caching is unavailable or not allowed,

00:37:31.680 --> 00:37:33.680
the query proceeds to the database.

00:37:33.680 --> 00:37:37.680
The database combines cached and uncached information,

00:37:37.680 --> 00:37:40.680
requesting data from the operating system.

00:37:40.680 --> 00:37:43.680
The speed of retrieving the data depends on available RAM

00:37:43.680 --> 00:37:49.680
and the storage type, with NVMe being the fastest and HDD being the slowest.

00:37:49.680 --> 00:37:53.680
Simple queries return data immediately to the PHP call,

00:37:53.680 --> 00:37:58.680
while complex queries requiring temp tables demand more processing time

00:37:58.680 --> 00:38:03.680
and can slow down the website or cause server issues under high traffic.

00:38:03.680 --> 00:38:07.680
Several tuning techniques can improve performance.

00:38:07.680 --> 00:38:10.680
You can set the buffer pool size.

00:38:10.680 --> 00:38:16.680
The buffer pool is the area of memory that MariaDB uses to cache data and indexes.

00:38:16.680 --> 00:38:20.680
It's important to configure the buffer pool size appropriately

00:38:20.680 --> 00:38:24.680
based on the amount of available memory and the size of the database.

00:38:24.680 --> 00:38:28.680
The buffer pool size can be controlled using the

00:38:28.680 --> 00:38:32.680
nodb underscore buffer underscore pool underscore size variable.

00:38:32.680 --> 00:38:36.680
You can start with 128 or 256 megabytes,

00:38:36.680 --> 00:38:39.680
depending on the total available RAM on your server.

00:38:39.680 --> 00:38:45.680
Check the hit rate and adjust accordingly to ensure it's working as expected.

00:38:45.680 --> 00:38:49.680
You can see here where the configuration files are,

00:38:49.680 --> 00:38:57.680
and always make sure to restart your services to apply any of your changes after you configure this.

00:38:57.680 --> 00:39:01.680
One way to analyze buffer pool performance is by observing

00:39:01.680 --> 00:39:06.680
the nodb underscore buffer underscore pool underscore weight underscore free status variable.

00:39:06.680 --> 00:39:09.680
If it's decreasing, then you don't have enough buffer pool,

00:39:09.680 --> 00:39:13.680
or your or your flushing isn't occurring frequently enough.

00:39:13.680 --> 00:39:19.680
In this case, you should set the nodb underscore buffer underscore pool underscore size variable higher

00:39:19.680 --> 00:39:22.680
if your system resources allows for it.

