data access and reduced latency compared to traditional storage solutions.

00:47:44.680 --> 00:47:49.680
This results in quicker response times and more efficient query processing

00:47:49.680 --> 00:47:52.680
leading to improved overall server performance.

00:47:52.680 --> 00:47:57.680
Disk I.O. helps increase performance of all aspects of your web server

00:47:57.680 --> 00:48:01.680
and having NVMe speeds usually means that the database caching

00:48:01.680 --> 00:48:04.680
in W3.12 cache plugin is not needed

00:48:04.680 --> 00:48:08.680
and it can have adverse effects to performance if enabled.

00:48:08.680 --> 00:48:14.680
NVMe storage improves server performance for WordPress websites in several ways.

00:48:14.680 --> 00:48:17.680
Faster page load times.

00:48:17.680 --> 00:48:22.680
With its high speed data transfer and load latency, NVMe storage allows

00:48:22.680 --> 00:48:28.680
for quicker access to website files and assets such as images, style sheets, and scripts.

00:48:28.680 --> 00:48:33.680
This leads to faster page load times and a better user experience.

00:48:33.680 --> 00:48:35.680
Improved database performance.

00:48:35.680 --> 00:48:39.680
The benefits of using NVMe storage when processing database queries

00:48:39.680 --> 00:48:42.680
can be outlined as follows.

00:48:42.680 --> 00:48:46.680
When processing queries, the database relies on both cached and uncached

00:48:46.680 --> 00:48:50.680
MySQL data requiring interactions with the operating system to retrieve

00:48:50.680 --> 00:48:52.680
the necessary information.

00:48:52.680 --> 00:48:55.680
If the operating system has ample RAM,

00:48:55.680 --> 00:48:59.680
the files needed for a query might already be stored in RAM

00:48:59.680 --> 00:49:03.680
rather than only being available on a persistent storage device.

00:49:03.680 --> 00:49:07.680
Finally, in cases where data must be accessed from a storage device,

00:49:07.680 --> 00:49:11.680
an NVMe drive provides significantly faster data retrieval

00:49:11.680 --> 00:49:16.680
compared to a SATA SSD while traditional HHDs are much slower

00:49:16.680 --> 00:49:19.680
and typically not used in such scenarios anymore.

00:49:19.680 --> 00:49:23.680
Overall, the NVMe storage greatly enhances database query performance

00:49:23.680 --> 00:49:28.680
due to its rapid data access capabilities.

00:49:28.680 --> 00:49:33.680
Compressing data server responses with Brotli or gzip can significantly improve

00:49:33.680 --> 00:49:37.680
website performance by reducing the amount of data transferred over the network.

00:49:37.680 --> 00:49:41.680
This provides a final stage of optimization on the response

00:49:41.680 --> 00:49:44.680
being returned from the origin server.

00:49:44.680 --> 00:49:51.680
On average, gzip compression can reduce the size of web content by 60 to 70 percent

00:49:51.680 --> 00:49:54.680
while Brotli tends to provide even better compression rates

00:49:54.680 --> 00:49:58.680
often reducing content size by 70 to 80 percent.

00:49:58.680 --> 00:50:02.680
Enabling Brotli compression within Nginx is accomplished

00:50:02.680 --> 00:50:06.680
through the use of the Brotli Nginx module.

00:50:06.680 --> 00:50:11.680
The module can be compiled and installed alongside Nginx and loaded dynamically.

00:50:11.680 --> 00:50:13.680
Analyze a server response for compression.

00:50:13.680 --> 00:50:19.680
To determine if a request response was compressed with Brotli or gzip compression

00:50:19.680 --> 00:50:24.680
you can use Chrome or Firefox developer tools to review the content encoding headers.

00:50:24.680 --> 00:50:26.680
So you can follow these steps.

00:50:26.680 --> 00:50:29.680
Open the developer tools in your browser.

00:50:29.680 --> 00:50:32.680
Click on the Network tab in the developer tools.

00:50:32.680 --> 00:50:37.680
Refresh the webpage or initiate the action you want to analyze.

00:50:37.680 --> 00:50:42.680
This will populate the network panel with a list of requests made by the browser.

00:50:42.680 --> 00:50:46.680
Then we'll find the requests we're interested in

00:50:46.680 --> 00:50:52.680
such as the main document request or a specific resource like a CSS or a JavaScript file.

00:50:52.680 --> 00:50:55.680
We'll click on the request to open the detailed view.

00:50:55.680 --> 00:50:59.680
In the detailed view we'll look for the Headers tab.

00:50:59.680 --> 00:51:02.680
This should be the default tab in both browsers.

00:51:02.680 --> 00:51:06.680
Scroll down to the Response Headers section.

00:51:06.680 --> 00:51:10.680
Then we'll check the value of the content encoding header.

00:51:10.680 --> 00:51:14.680
If this response is compressed with gzip it will display gzip

00:51:14.680 --> 00:51:18.680
and if it's compressed with Brotli it will display br.

00:51:18.680 --> 00:51:21.680
If the header is absent or has a different value

00:51:21.680 --> 00:51:24.680
the response is not compressed using either of these algorithms.

00:51:24.680 --> 00:51:28.680
By examining the content encoding header in the developer tools

00:51:28.680 --> 00:51:36.680
you can quickly determine if a request response was compressed with Brotli or gzip.

00:51:36.680 --> 00:51:42.680
Serving requests from the NGINX CAS can result in significant performance improvements

00:51:42.680 --> 00:51:46.680
as it eliminates the need for the request to traverse the entire stack

00:51:46.680 --> 00:51:49.680
and access the backend services.

00:51:49.680 --> 00:51:53.680
This can lead to reduced processing times and improved response times

00:51:53.680 --> 00:51:55.680
resulting in a better user experience.

00:51:55.680 --> 00:51:59.680
According to benchmarks serving requests from the NGINX CAS

00:51:59.680 --> 00:52:03.680
can result in a 90% reduction in response times

00:52:03.680 --> 00:52:06.680
compared to requests that traverse the entire stack.

00:52:06.680 --> 00:52:11.680
This is due to the cache mechanism's ability to serve requests

00:52:11.680 --> 00:52:16.680
directly from memory or disk reducing the time taken to process the request.

00:52:16.680 --> 00:52:19.680
Furthermore serving requests from the NGINX CAS

00:52:19.680 --> 00:52:23.680
can significantly reduce server load and improve scalability.

00:52:23.680 --> 00:52:27.680
By reducing the number of requests that must be handled by backend services

00:52:27.680 --> 00:52:29.680
like PHP, FPM or MySQL

00:52:29.680 --> 00:52:32.680
the server can handle more requests concurrently

00:52:32.680 --> 00:52:36.680
leading to improved performance and faster response times.

00:52:36.680 --> 00:52:39.680
This is essential for a high-traffic website.

00:52:39.680 --> 00:52:46.680
The UltraStack NGINX configuration adds an x-proxy cache HTTP header to responses

00:52:46.680 --> 00:52:51.680
and the upstream cache status to NGINX access log entries.

00:52:51.680 --> 00:52:56.680
The following three techniques explain how to identify whether the page being received

00:52:56.680 --> 00:53:00.680
is a cache version served by NGINX.

00:53:00.680 --> 00:53:02.680
We can use curl.

00:53:02.680 --> 00:53:05.680
We can make a simple get request to the URL in question

00:53:05.680 --> 00:53:09.680
and use grep to check the x-proxy cache header.

00:53:09.680 --> 00:53:11.680
Also we can use chrome dev tools.

00:53:11.680 --> 00:53:15.680
You can use F12 or the other shortcut to bring up the dev tools window

00:53:15.680 --> 00:53:16.680
then click the network tab.

00:53:16.680 --> 00:53:19.680
Pick the box that says disable cache

00:53:19.680 --> 00:53:22.680
which will bypass chrome's internal cache

00:53:22.680 --> 00:53:26.680
then reload the page to capture all of the network information.

00:53:26.680 --> 00:53:30.680
Not disabling chrome's cache will allow you to see 304

00:53:30.680 --> 00:53:33.680
not modified responses for static assets

00:53:33.680 --> 00:53:36.680
which shows how the client side cache is working.

00:53:36.680 --> 00:53:41.680
The following are the possible values for upstream cache status.

00:53:41.680 --> 00:53:44.680
Miss the response was not found in the cache

00:53:44.680 --> 00:53:47.680
and so was fetched from an origin server.

00:53:47.680 --> 00:53:50.680
The response might then have been cached.

00:53:50.680 --> 00:53:54.680
Bypass the response was fetched from the origin server

00:53:54.680 --> 00:53:59.680
fetched from the cache because the response matched a proxy cache bypass directive.

00:53:59.680 --> 00:54:02.680
Expired. The entry in the cache has expired.

00:54:02.680 --> 00:54:06.680
The response contains fresh content from the origin server.

00:54:06.680 --> 00:54:10.680
stale. The content is stale because the origin server is not responding correctly

00:54:10.680 --> 00:54:14.680
and proxy cache use stale was configured.

00:54:14.680 --> 00:54:19.680
Updating. The content is stale because the entry is currently being updated

00:54:19.680 --> 00:54:21.680
in response to a previous request

00:54:21.680 --> 00:54:25.680
and proxy cache use stale updating is configured.

00:54:25.680 --> 00:54:29.680
Revalidated. The proxy cache revalidate

00:54:29.680 --> 00:54:35.680
directive was enabled and nginx verified that the current cache content was still valid.

00:54:35.680 --> 00:54:39.680
Hit. The response contains valid, fresh content

00:54:39.680 --> 00:54:43.680
direct from the cache. Another technique to explain how to identify whether

00:54:43.680 --> 00:54:47.680
the page being received is the cache version served by nginx

00:54:47.680 --> 00:54:51.680
is using the nginx access log. You connect to the

00:54:51.680 --> 00:54:55.680
server using SSH and read the access log with a tail or a

00:54:55.680 --> 00:54:59.680
cat. The value assigned to UCS or the

00:54:59.680 --> 00:55:03.680
upstream cache status will be one of the cache statuses we talked about before.

00:55:03.680 --> 00:55:07.680
Using central's managed server tools, here's how you would enable nginx

00:55:07.680 --> 00:55:11.680
proxy cache on your site. With nginx proxy cache configured

00:55:11.680 --> 00:55:15.680
you may use the ultra stack utilities to

00:55:15.680 --> 00:55:19.680
determine the cache hit rates for all requests to nginx.

00:55:19.680 --> 00:55:23.680
While this is a tool specific to in-motion hosting

00:55:23.680 --> 00:55:27.680
the information can be received using the third party nginx module

00:55:27.680 --> 00:55:31.680
VTS. So in summary, using nginx proxy cache will help

00:55:31.680 --> 00:55:35.680
drive performance for high traffic websites.

00:55:35.680 --> 00:55:39.680
While the exact amount depends on the specific

00:55:39.680 --> 00:55:43.680
workload of the website, a minimum of 4 GB and

00:55:43.680 --> 00:55:47.680
2 vCPU cores is recommended when benchmarking your

00:55:47.680 --> 00:55:51.680
resource needs on a high traffic website. In a high performance

00:55:51.680 --> 00:55:55.680
server running MySQL, Redis, PHP, FPM, Apache, and

00:55:55.680 --> 00:55:59.680
nginx, having enough RAM is critical to allow each services

00:55:59.680 --> 00:56:03.680
caching techniques to work at their full potential. These services use

00:56:03.680 --> 00:56:07.680
RAM to cache frequently accessed data and reduce the time taken

00:56:07.680 --> 00:56:11.680
to retrieve it from disk or other storage devices. If the server

00:56:11.680 --> 00:56:15.680
does not have enough RAM, the caching will not be as effective, leading

00:56:15.680 --> 00:56:19.680
to longer processing times and slower website performance.

00:56:19.680 --> 00:56:23.680
For instance, MySQL uses the NODB buffer pool to

00:56:23.680 --> 00:56:27.680
cache frequently accessed data from the database, and Redis uses

00:56:27.680 --> 00:56:31.680
memory to store frequently accessed data in key value pairs.

00:56:31.680 --> 00:56:35.680
PHP FPM uses opcache to cache PHP

00:56:35.680 --> 00:56:39.680
scripts, and nginx uses RAM to cache frequently requested

00:56:39.680 --> 00:56:43.680
webpages and assets. If the server does not have sufficient

00:56:43.680 --> 00:56:47.680
RAM, these caching techniques will not be as effective, and the

00:56:47.680 --> 00:56:51.680
server may resort to swapping data in and out of slower

00:56:51.680 --> 00:56:55.680
storage devices, leading to a slower response times and reduced server

00:56:55.680 --> 00:56:59.680
capacity. Swap usage. To determine if a

00:56:59.680 --> 00:57:03.680
Linux server is swapping, you can use the free command to view the system's

00:57:03.680 --> 00:57:07.680
memory usage. If the swap value is greater than 0,

00:57:07.680 --> 00:57:11.680
it indicates that the server is currently using swap space to store

00:57:11.680 --> 00:57:15.680
data in memory. Additionally, you can use the vmstat command

00:57:15.680 --> 00:57:19.680
to monitor the amount of swapping activity occurring on the server,

00:57:19.680 --> 00:57:23.680
with the SI and SO values indicating the amount of memory

00:57:23.680 --> 00:57:27.680
being swapped in and out, respectively. This example

00:57:27.680 --> 00:57:31.680
does not use swap and is not swapping. OOM

00:57:31.680 --> 00:57:35.680
OOM kills. To determine if a Linux server is experiencing

00:57:35.680 --> 00:57:39.680
OOM, OOM or out of memory kills, you can check

00:57:39.680 --> 00:57:43.680
the system logs for messages related to OOM events.

00:57:43.680 --> 00:57:47.680
On most Linux distributions, the system log file is located

00:57:47.680 --> 00:57:51.680
at var slash log slash messages, or var slash log slash syslog.

00:57:51.680 --> 00:57:55.680
To view OOM related messages, you can search for

00:57:55.680 --> 00:57:59.680
the keyword OOM using the grep command.

00:57:59.680 --> 00:58:03.680
If OOM or OOM events are occurring, you'll see

00:58:03.680 --> 00:58:07.680
entries that indicate which process or application was killed by

00:58:07.680 --> 00:58:11.680
the OOM killer. Additionally, you can use tools like

00:58:11.680 --> 00:58:15.680
DMESG and journal control to view kernel messages

00:58:15.680 --> 00:58:19.680
related to OOM events. By having enough RAM on the server,

00:58:19.680 --> 00:58:23.680
each service can utilize caching techniques to their fullest extent,

00:58:23.680 --> 00:58:27.680
resulting in faster processing time, reduced

00:58:27.680 --> 00:58:33.680
disk usage, and an overall better user experience.

