most programmers tend to use spacing and comments to make code readable for both

01:22:41.680 --> 01:22:45.680
themselves and others. Minification is a technique used to reduce

01:22:45.680 --> 01:22:49.680
load times on a website by removing unnecessary characters and comments from the

01:22:49.680 --> 01:22:53.680
code for faster delivery and rendering. This technique can be used

01:22:53.680 --> 01:22:57.680
on HTML, CSS, and JavaScript files and can dramatically

01:22:57.680 --> 01:23:01.680
improve your site's performance. To minify your assets with W3 Total

01:23:01.680 --> 01:23:05.680
Cache, you'll need to enable it in general settings. There's an automatic mode which attempts

01:23:05.680 --> 01:23:09.680
to handle the heavy lifting for you, but if you have issues with it, you'll need to

01:23:09.680 --> 01:23:13.680
enable manual mode and add the assets you wish to be minified yourself under

01:23:13.680 --> 01:23:17.680
the minify page. There is a help wizard for setting up

01:23:17.680 --> 01:23:21.680
minify, which you can use to specify the template to use when loading the asset.

01:23:21.680 --> 01:23:25.680
Five,

01:23:25.680 --> 01:23:29.680
concatenation. The term concatenation refers to the action of linking

01:23:29.680 --> 01:23:33.680
things together in a series. In the context of a website performance,

01:23:33.680 --> 01:23:37.680
this means combining multiple CSS and JavaScript files into a few files

01:23:37.680 --> 01:23:41.680
to deliver the assets the browser needs faster with less requests.

01:23:41.680 --> 01:23:45.680
In W3 Total Cache, concatenating files is an option

01:23:45.680 --> 01:23:49.680
in general settings under minify. Checking the box will allow you to combine

01:23:49.680 --> 01:23:53.680
the asset files into one of three different files in the DOM.

01:23:53.680 --> 01:23:57.680
One in the head, one in the body, or one at the footer before

01:23:57.680 --> 01:24:01.680
closing the document. Six, eliminate render blocking resources.

01:24:01.680 --> 01:24:05.680
Render blocking resources is essentially coding your website files,

01:24:05.680 --> 01:24:09.680
usually CSS and JavaScript, that prevents a webpage from loading quickly.

01:24:09.680 --> 01:24:13.680
To ensure your site loads as efficiently as possible, you can specify

01:24:13.680 --> 01:24:17.680
attributes that tell the browser how to download the code in relation to the other

01:24:17.680 --> 01:24:21.680
site assets. When concatenating and minifying files

01:24:21.680 --> 01:24:25.680
using W3 Total Cache, you also have the option of embedding them before

01:24:25.680 --> 01:24:29.680
the the close of the document's head tag, or after the body tag.

01:24:29.680 --> 01:24:33.680
To improve performance, you have the option of setting rel attributes

01:24:33.680 --> 01:24:37.680
for the assets such as async or defer, which will tell the browser

01:24:37.680 --> 01:24:41.680
when and how to load the file. The minify wizard in Total Cache

01:24:41.680 --> 01:24:45.680
will allow you to specify which JS and CSS files load by template,

01:24:45.680 --> 01:24:49.680
allowing you to have to have fine tune control over which assets load

01:24:49.680 --> 01:24:53.680
when and where. Seven, preconnect and prefetch

01:24:53.680 --> 01:24:57.680
DNS hints. If your site is using multiple third party scripts, the

01:24:57.680 --> 01:25:01.680
location those scripts are hosted on will be considered a cross

01:25:01.680 --> 01:25:05.680
origin domain. That third party domain will need to be resolved to an IP

01:25:05.680 --> 01:25:09.680
address for the browser to download the file and complete the request. The time it

01:25:09.680 --> 01:25:13.680
takes for this DNS resolution to take place can introduce latency

01:25:13.680 --> 01:25:17.680
into the page load. And using preconnect and prefetch will instruct the browser to

01:25:17.680 --> 01:25:21.680
handle this prior to fetching the asset. This can help remove the latency

01:25:21.680 --> 01:25:25.680
introduced by resolving a large number of DNS requests for assets in the background.

01:25:25.680 --> 01:25:29.680
Using DNS prefetch and preconnect

01:25:29.680 --> 01:25:33.680
links will help your page load faster by performing these operations

01:25:33.680 --> 01:25:37.680
for later use. The DNS prefetch will do a DNS

01:25:37.680 --> 01:25:41.680
lookup while preconnect will establish the connection to the server.

01:25:41.680 --> 01:25:45.680
This connection established will also include a TLS handshake

01:25:45.680 --> 01:25:49.680
if served over HTTPS. The preconnect hint

01:25:49.680 --> 01:25:53.680
should be used sparingly and only for the most critical assets being used in the viewport

01:25:53.680 --> 01:25:57.680
during the initial page load. For non-critical assets, using

01:25:57.680 --> 01:26:01.680
only the DNS prefetch hint is recommended.

01:26:01.680 --> 01:26:05.680
Eight, image optimization. Image optimization refers to

01:26:05.680 --> 01:26:09.680
the practice of a website delivering the highest quality image with the right format

01:26:09.680 --> 01:26:13.680
with the right format, resolution, and dimensions for the

01:26:13.680 --> 01:26:17.680
device and viewport accessing them. This is done with the

01:26:17.680 --> 01:26:21.680
intent of keeping the file size as small as possible. There are several layers

01:26:21.680 --> 01:26:25.680
when dealing with optimizing your images, including removing metadata,

01:26:25.680 --> 01:26:29.680
converting to a different format, resizing, and lazy loading. One of the ways to

01:26:29.680 --> 01:26:33.680
reduce an image's file size is to remove any metadata attached to the file.

01:26:33.680 --> 01:26:37.680
This static can include information about the camera used to take the picture,

01:26:37.680 --> 01:26:41.680
GPS coordinates, the file's owners, comments, a thumbnail, and more.

01:26:41.680 --> 01:26:45.680
There are several types of metadata that can be added to the images

01:26:45.680 --> 01:26:49.680
including EXIF, IPTC, XMP.

01:26:49.680 --> 01:26:53.680
Removing this data will help to decrease the file size of the image.

01:26:53.680 --> 01:26:57.680
An added benefit is that removing metadata keeps these details from being

01:26:57.680 --> 01:27:01.680
publicly shared. WebP is a compression format

01:27:01.680 --> 01:27:05.680
for images developed by Google. When optimizing your images

01:27:05.680 --> 01:27:09.680
for speed, using WebP is the recommended format for websites.

01:27:09.680 --> 01:27:13.680
Most modern browsers now support the WebP image format. There are many WordPress

01:27:13.680 --> 01:27:17.680
plugins that offer this type of image conversion and most even assist with

01:27:17.680 --> 01:27:21.680
redirects to automate the process and a fallback option in case the browser

01:27:21.680 --> 01:27:25.680
does not support the WebP format. W3 Total Cache has a service for

01:27:25.680 --> 01:27:29.680
conversion of images and redirects under media library when

01:27:29.680 --> 01:27:33.680
image service extension is activated. Dynamic image resizing.

01:27:33.680 --> 01:27:37.680
When different devices are used to access your webpage, the image size

01:27:37.680 --> 01:27:41.680
needed will vary depending on the visitor's viewport. Dynamic image sizes

01:27:41.680 --> 01:27:45.680
can be used to make each of these devices load the image with a size

01:27:45.680 --> 01:27:49.680
tailored specifically to the viewport where the site is being rendered.

01:27:49.680 --> 01:27:53.680
A great example of this would be the hero image on your website. While a desktop would

01:27:53.680 --> 01:27:57.680
likely need the full size version of the image, the

01:27:57.680 --> 01:28:01.680
narrow viewport of a mobile device may only need an image that is 20%

01:28:01.680 --> 01:28:05.680
of that size. Providing the browser with the smaller size would reduce

01:28:05.680 --> 01:28:09.680
the time it needs to download the file and display it. There are many WordPress

01:28:09.680 --> 01:28:13.680
plugins and CDN providers that offer dynamic resizing for images.

01:28:13.680 --> 01:28:17.680
The solution you choose could be based on your audience. If your visitors

01:28:17.680 --> 01:28:21.680
are mostly local and your traffic is not high volume, a CDN's cost

01:28:21.680 --> 01:28:25.680
for this service may outweigh the benefits to you, so using a WordPress plugin may be

01:28:25.680 --> 01:28:29.680
your best option. 9 Lazy loading images and other

01:28:29.680 --> 01:28:33.680
embeds. Lazy loading is the practice of identifying non-critical

01:28:33.680 --> 01:28:37.680
assets and ensuring they only load later as needed to render

01:28:37.680 --> 01:28:41.680
the page in the browser. In the context of images, lazy loading is usually

01:28:41.680 --> 01:28:45.680
based on a user interaction such as scrolling, and loads

01:28:45.680 --> 01:28:49.680
images needed as the user gets near them. Lazy loading allows the first render

01:28:49.680 --> 01:28:53.680
to happen faster, as it instructs the browser to only load the critical

01:28:53.680 --> 01:28:57.680
resources needed to display what is being rendered in the viewport.

01:28:57.680 --> 01:29:01.680
Then, as you scroll, the application can detect when resources are

01:29:01.680 --> 01:29:05.680
needed and initialize the download. W3TrollCache offers lazy

01:29:05.680 --> 01:29:09.680
loading for images. Finally, using image facades for interactive

01:29:09.680 --> 01:29:13.680
elements. One of the biggest issues authors run across is having

01:29:13.680 --> 01:29:17.680
multiple video embeds from providers such as YouTube or Vimeo.

01:29:17.680 --> 01:29:21.680
Loading these videos will force your site to do DNS lookups,

01:29:21.680 --> 01:29:25.680
make connections to these third-party servers in the background, and download

01:29:25.680 --> 01:29:29.680
the elements needed to render the content. Providing an image that is

01:29:29.680 --> 01:29:33.680
stirred from your site's domain as an interaction point to trigger loading these types

01:29:33.680 --> 01:29:37.680
of content embeds can save you precious time rendering your webpage.

01:29:37.680 --> 01:29:41.680
It also saves a user from downloading JavaScript and CSS for an

01:29:41.680 --> 01:29:45.680
element they are not going to use, as the video does not render unless the user

01:29:45.680 --> 01:29:49.680
specifically clicks on the image anchor link to trigger it. This is also

01:29:49.680 --> 01:29:53.680
true for other interactive elements such as chat embeds or other interactive elements

01:29:53.680 --> 01:29:57.680
that are not hosted on your server and are being served to your visitor

01:29:57.680 --> 01:30:01.680
from a remote service.

01:30:01.680 --> 01:30:05.680
Okay, now that we've done some optimization

01:30:05.680 --> 01:30:09.680
to our WordPress page, let's enter the WordPress URL in the

01:30:09.680 --> 01:30:13.680
page speed insight and analyze it again.

01:30:13.680 --> 01:30:17.680
And we can

01:30:17.680 --> 01:30:21.680
see the numbers are better. Our performance is up

01:30:21.680 --> 01:30:25.680
and our performance is up on mobile

01:30:25.680 --> 01:30:29.680
and our performance is up on desktop. This was a basic

01:30:29.680 --> 01:30:33.680
overview for configuring your performance. And there are additional

01:30:33.680 --> 01:30:37.680
ways to drill down into your configuration when aiming for higher scores.

01:30:37.680 --> 01:30:41.680
A great example of this is using custom page templates for your most visited

01:30:41.680 --> 01:30:45.680
and important landing pages and using the minify help wizard to

01:30:45.680 --> 01:30:49.680
only load the critical CSS and JavaScript assets on those pages

01:30:49.680 --> 01:30:53.680
if they're needed. Some plugins like contact forms can load their resources

01:30:53.680 --> 01:30:57.680
on all pages, even if there's not a contact form embedded.

01:30:57.680 --> 01:31:01.680
You've reached the end of the course, you've learned about key metrics important to

01:31:01.680 --> 01:31:05.680
website performance, and you've learned a bunch of things you can do to

01:31:05.680 --> 01:31:29.680
optimize the performance of your website. Thanks for watching.

